---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { SERVICES } from '../../data/services';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Services', path: '/services/' }];
const quickAnswer = 'Trusted Termite and Pest Solutions offers recurring pest control, scorpion exclusion, termite treatment, rodent and wildlife work, bed bug and mosquito treatment, lawn care and weed control, brush and lot clearing, and junk removal — all from one licensed owner-operator based in Seminole, Texas.';
const groups = [
  { key: 'pest', title: 'Pest control' },
  { key: 'termite', title: 'Termite' },
  { key: 'lawn', title: 'Lawn, weeds and brush' },
  { key: 'junk', title: 'Junk and property' },
] as const;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Our Services | Trusted Termite &amp; Pest Solutions"
  description="Pest control, scorpions, termites, rodents, bed bugs, mosquitoes, lawn care, brush clearing and junk removal across Gaines County and the Permian Basin."
  path="/services/"
  altLangPath="/es/servicios/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">One licensed operator, four lines of work</p>
      <h1>What we do</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <p class="measure" style="margin-top:1rem"><a href="/es/servicios/" lang="es" hreflang="es">Esta página en español</a></p>
      {groups.map(g => (
        <div style="margin-top:2.4rem">
          <h2>{g.title}</h2>
          <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1rem">
            {SERVICES.filter(s => s.group === g.key).map(s => (
              <a class="card" href={`/services/${s.slug}/`}>
                <h3>{s.name}</h3><p>{s.summary}</p>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}

      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:3rem">
        <h2>Why one person covers four different trades</h2>
        <p>
          The list above looks broader than a one-man business ought to be able to cover, so it is worth
          explaining how it is actually structured, because the answer is not "we will try anything."
        </p>
        <p>
          Texas licenses structural pest control by category rather than issuing a single general
          permit. The four categories held here — pest control, termite, lawn and ornamental, and weed
          control — are separately examined and separately maintained, and between them they define the
          legal boundary of what this business can do. Everything in the pest, termite and lawn columns
          above sits inside one of those four categories. Nothing on this site is offered outside them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The property work — brush and lot clearing, junk removal — is not licensed pest work at all,
          and it is on the list for a practical reason rather than an opportunistic one. A very large
          share of the pest problems on properties in this region are harbourage problems: brush against
          the structure, a lot that has not been cut, a pile of scrap metal or stacked lumber that has
          been sitting since the last owner. Treating a house whose problem is the twenty feet of
          overgrowth beside it is a treatment aimed at the symptom, and being able to remove the cause
          in the same week rather than telling you to find somebody else is why the service exists.
        </p>
        <p>
          What that does not mean: we are not a landscaping company, a demolition contractor, a
          dumpster rental service or a pressure washing outfit. Those requests come in and they get
          declined. The line is drawn at work that either requires the licence or directly removes a
          pest condition, and it is drawn deliberately rather than case by case.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">Which service you actually need</h2>
        <p>
          Most people call knowing what they saw rather than knowing what to ask for, which is fine —
          describing the symptom is more useful than guessing the category. But if you want to arrive at
          the conversation with a sense of where you are, here is how the lines divide.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You are seeing insects inside and you do not know what they are.</strong> That is
          general pest control, and it is the starting point for the majority of calls. The identification
          happens during the inspection, and it matters, because ants, roaches and occasional invaders
          look interchangeable to most people and are treated very differently.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You are seeing scorpions.</strong> That is its own service and its own approach.
          Scorpion work is an exclusion and harbourage discipline far more than a spraying discipline,
          and a general perimeter treatment applied without addressing where they are living and how
          they are entering will disappoint you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You have mud tubes, discarded wings, or wood that sounds hollow.</strong> Termite, and
          it needs an inspection rather than a phone quote. This is also the one where the honest answer
          is sometimes that what you are describing is not termites at all, which is a better outcome
          for you and a worse one for our invoice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You are hearing something in the ceiling or finding droppings.</strong> Rodent work, and
          the answer is almost never bait alone. Rodent control that does not close the entry is a
          renewable subscription rather than a solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Something larger has moved in under the shed or into the attic.</strong> Wildlife, which
          is a different set of methods and a different set of legal constraints depending on the animal.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Your lot has been cited, or the weeds are past your knees.</strong> Weed control or
          brush and lot clearing, depending on whether the answer is chemical or mechanical. If a city
          notice is involved, say so first — it has a date on it and the date drives everything.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The problem is a pile of something.</strong> Junk removal, and it is worth combining
          with a pest service rather than doing separately, because moving a long-standing pile
          reliably disturbs whatever has been living under it.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">How the services fit together on a real property</h2>
        <p>
          Very few properties out here need exactly one of these things, and the sequencing matters more
          than most people expect.
        </p>
        <p>
          The general order is: remove the harbourage, close the entry, then treat. Doing it in the
          reverse order — treating first, clearing later — means the treatment is applied to a property
          that is about to be substantially disturbed, and the disturbance pushes the population around
          in ways that make the result impossible to read. If a lot is going to be cleared, clearing it
          first and treating afterwards is worth the extra week.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exception is termite work, which is not affected by any of that and should be dealt with on
          its own schedule as soon as it is identified. Termite activity is the one thing on this list
          where waiting has a compounding cost.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rodent work sits in the middle. Exclusion — closing the gaps, sealing the penetrations, fixing
          the door sweep and the roll-up threshold — is the durable part, and trapping without it simply
          creates vacancies. Where exclusion needs a hole closed that we are not the right trade to
          close, you will be told that rather than having a foam gun waved at a structural problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lawn and weed work interacts with all of it. A treated perimeter running through knee-high
          vegetation is not a perimeter. Where the yard is the problem, addressing the yard is part of
          the pest plan rather than an upsell attached to it.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">A year of work across all of it</h2>
        <p>
          Pest pressure in West Texas is strongly seasonal, and the calendar is more predictable here
          than most people realise. Knowing roughly what is coming is the difference between scheduling
          work and reacting to it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Late winter into early spring.</strong> The quietest stretch, and the best time to do
          the work that benefits from being done before pressure arrives — exclusion, perimeter
          establishment, lot clearing while the vegetation is down, and termite inspections ahead of
          swarm season. Scheduling is easiest in this window and it is the least popular time to call,
          which is exactly why it is the most useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Spring.</strong> Termite swarms, ants becoming active, weeds moving fast after any
          rain, and the first scorpion calls. This is when city weed enforcement starts generating
          notices, and when a lot that looked fine in February stops looking fine within about three
          weeks.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Early summer.</strong> The heaviest general pest period. Ants, roaches, spiders,
          crickets, and scorpion activity climbing steadily. Mosquito pressure follows any standing
          water, which in this region means it follows rain events rather than running continuously.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>High summer.</strong> Peak scorpion season and the busiest weeks of the year.
          Everything is looking for moisture, which means everything is moving toward structures. This is
          the period when calling a day or two ahead genuinely changes what slot you get.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Late summer into harvest.</strong> Agricultural activity around the region pushes
          rodents and field insects toward buildings. Properties adjacent to fields see this sharply and
          suddenly, and it is one of the most reliably predictable events on the calendar.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Autumn.</strong> The rodent season proper. First cold nights move mice and rats into
          structures, and the buildings that get hit are the ones with the gaps that were there all along.
          Exclusion done in September is worth several visits in December.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Winter.</strong> Quieter, but not empty. Rodents already inside stay inside, and this is
          a good period for the deliberate work — junk removal, clearing, and the structural fixes that
          nobody wants to do in August heat.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">What every first visit includes</h2>
        <p>
          Regardless of which service brought us out, the first visit follows the same shape, because the
          inspection is not a formality attached to the job — it is the part that determines whether the
          job works.
        </p>
        <p>
          A full exterior walk of the structure, not just the side where you saw the problem. Utility
          penetrations, weep holes, door sweeps, thresholds, the garage, the roofline where it can be
          seen, and the ground contact points. Then the drainage and moisture story: irrigation, downspouts,
          condensate lines, and anywhere water reliably arrives.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then whatever else is on the property. Outbuildings get looked at if they exist, because on
          acreage they are usually where the population actually lives and the house is merely where it is
          noticed. Fence lines, stored material and vegetation against the structure all get read as part
          of the same picture.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then the interior where it is relevant — the rooms where you are seeing activity and the rooms
          next to them, which is generally where the explanation is.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then a conversation before anything is applied, covering what was found, what it means, what is
          going to be done about it, what it will cost, and what you can do yourself that would help.
          That last item is not filler. On most properties there are two or three free changes that do
          more for the outcome than an additional treatment would.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">Where these services are available</h2>
        <p>
          Everything on this page is available across the service area, which runs out from Seminole
          through Gaines County and across the Permian Basin and southern South Plains. What changes with
          distance is not what can be done but how the scheduling works.
        </p>
        <p>
          Seminole and the towns immediately around it have the most flexibility, including the ability
          to respond quickly when something genuinely cannot wait. The further towns run as planned days
          rather than on demand, which means a call with a day or two of notice gets a far better slot
          than one placed the same morning. That is a consequence of one truck covering real distances,
          and it is better said out loud than discovered.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical advice is the same everywhere: if you have a deadline — a closing, a lease
          turnover, a city notice with a date on it, an inspection — lead with it. Deadlines drive
          scheduling more than anything else, and a week of notice on a dated obligation is worth more
          than any amount of urgency on the day.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are outside the towns listed and not sure whether you are covered, call and ask rather
          than assuming. The map has edges, but they are not sharp, and a property just beyond a listed
          town is usually still workable on the right day.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">One-time service or a recurring schedule</h2>
        <p>
          This is the question that most affects what you spend over a year, and the industry has a
          reputation for answering it in its own favour, so here is the honest version.
        </p>
        <p>
          A one-time treatment is the right answer when the problem is an event rather than a condition.
          Something came in, it is being dealt with, and the property does not have the characteristics
          that would bring it back. A wasp nest, an isolated invasion after a rain, a single structure
          that has just been cleared and closed up. Paying for a schedule to handle a one-off is money
          spent on reassurance rather than on outcome.
        </p>
        <p>
          A recurring schedule is the right answer when the property has ongoing pressure that is not
          going away — which, in this region, describes most properties. Adjacency to open field or
          agriculture. Acreage with outbuildings. Older construction with a lot of entry points.
          Anywhere with sustained scorpion activity. In those cases the recurring option is not the
          upsell, it is the cheaper path, because the alternative is a series of one-time treatments at
          full visit cost each time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which one you get recommended depends on what the inspection found. If your property is a
          straightforward in-town house with a contained problem, you will be told that a single
          treatment should handle it, and that is the end of the conversation. Nobody is going to work
          around to a plan by describing what might be inside your walls.
        </p>
        <p>
          Schedules run bi-monthly or quarterly. Bi-monthly suits sustained pressure — acreage, older
          structures, properties on the edge of town. Quarterly suits most in-town houses. There is no
          contract on either, no term, no cancellation fee, and interim visits between scheduled ones are
          included rather than negotiated.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">Who does the work</h2>
        <p>
          Every service listed here is performed by the owner. Not dispatched to him, not supervised by
          him on paper while somebody else holds the sprayer — performed by him. The licence, the
          inspection, the application and the phone number are all attached to the same person, which is
          unusual enough in this trade that it is worth stating on the page that lists what is offered.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical effect is continuity. What you described in March is known in September, because
          there is no route sheet and no handover for it to fall through. The person who quoted the job
          is the person who finds out whether the quote was right, which is a strong incentive to quote
          it accurately the first time.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the business grows past what one truck can carry, the answer will be hiring and training
          properly rather than compressing more stops into the same day. Rushed service to protect route
          density is the failure mode this company was built to avoid, and it would be a strange thing to
          adopt as a reward for success.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">Commercial and rental property</h2>
        <p>
          The services above apply to commercial premises and rental property as much as to owner-occupied
          houses, with a few practical differences worth knowing before you call.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are <a href="/commercial/">dedicated pages for the six commercial types we actually work</a>. Commercial work usually needs paperwork sorted before anyone comes on site. A certificate of
          insurance naming your entity is the common one, and it takes minutes to arrange in advance and
          costs a visit if it is discovered on the day. Food-handling premises have their own
          documentation expectations, and the service record matters more there than almost anywhere
          else, because it is the thing an inspector asks to see.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rental property brings a coordination problem rather than a technical one: the person paying,
          the person authorising and the person opening the door are frequently three different people.
          That is entirely normal and it does not slow the work down, provided it is sorted before the
          visit rather than discovered at the gate. Tell us on the first call who is who.
        </p>
        <p>
          Multi-property owners are better served scheduling addresses together. Several properties in
          the same town on the same day is materially cheaper than the same properties treated as
          unrelated calls across three weeks, and it is less administration for everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Turnover work — treating between tenants — is worth planning rather than reacting to. An empty
          unit is the easiest possible property to treat properly, with full access, nothing to work
          around and no occupancy constraints on what can be applied. The window between tenants is
          short and it is the best opportunity a rental property gets all year.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.4rem">What we do not do</h2>
        <p>
          A short list, published deliberately, because knowing where a company stops is more informative
          than reading about everything it claims to start.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Fumigation.</strong> Requires its own certification, which is not held here. If a
          structure genuinely needs tenting, you need a company that does it, and you will be told so
          rather than sold something adjacent.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bed bug heat treatment.</strong> Heat is a specific method requiring specific equipment.
          Bed bug work is handled by conventional means here; where heat is the right answer, that is a
          different provider.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Landscaping, dumpster rental, pressure washing and demolition.</strong> Adjacent trades
          that get asked about constantly because the clearing and removal work looks like a doorway into
          them. It is not one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Anything requiring a licence category that is not held.</strong> The four categories on
          the licence are the boundary, and the licence number is published on this site precisely so that
          the boundary can be checked rather than taken on trust.
        </p>
        <p>
          Declining work is not a business strategy anybody would choose in a first year. It is, however,
          the only way the rest of the site means anything, and it is a good deal less expensive than
          learning a trade on somebody's property.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Cta />
</BaseLayout>
